{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 21,
  "title": "Fruit",
  "description": "Name the fruit, and get the plural right.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Fruit is a small, closed set of nouns that a learner meets very early, because it is what is in the kitchen and on the table. Nearly all of them are feminine.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The nouns",
        "body": "La manzana, la naranja, la sandía, la fresa, la cereza and la pera are all feminine. El plátano, el melón and el limón are masculine. La sandía is a good one to remember: it ends in -ía and keeps the feminine article, while el melón is masculine.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "la manzana",
            "en": "the apple"
          },
          {
            "es": "el melón",
            "en": "the melon"
          },
          {
            "es": "la sandía",
            "en": "the watermelon"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "One grape and many",
        "body": "Most fruit just adds -s or -es in the plural: las manzanas, los plátanos, las cerezas. One word does not: la uva becomes las uvas, and the -a drops. It is the commonest irregular plural in the language, and the only one you meet at this level.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "las uvas",
            "en": "the grapes"
          },
          {
            "es": "una uva",
            "en": "one grape"
          },
          {
            "es": "las fresas son buenas",
            "en": "the strawberries are good"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
